.TH man 7 "06 Dec 2024" "v4" "LDMS Plugin dynamic_stream_sampler man page"

.SH NAME
Plugin_dynamic_stream_sampler - man page for the LDMS dynamic_stream_sampler plugin

.SH SYNOPSIS
Within ldmsd_controller or a configuration file:
.br
config name=dynamic_stream_sampler [ <attr>=<value> ]

.SH DESCRIPTION
With LDMS (Lightweight Distributed Metric Service), plugins for the ldmsd (ldms daemon) are configured via ldmsd_controller
or a configuration file. The dynamic_stream_sampler plugin does not actually sample, but rather subscribes to an ldmsd_stream that is meant to take commands. When it gets a request on its command stream to subscribe to a new stream, it will (1) call subscribe on its ldmsd from the upstream L1 and (2) it will tell its upstream L1 via the command stream to subscribe to that stream from L2.

The request to subscribe will come from the app who is publishing to the stream already. Currently,functionality on the callback on the L0 on the command stream will block if it is sending a message to the L1 on the same command stream. For this current test, each ldmsd will be waiting for commands on a different command stream.

.SH CONFIGURATION ATTRIBUTE SYNTAX
The dynamic_stream_sampler plugin uses the sampler_base base class. This man page covers only the configuration attributes, or those with default values, specific to the this plugin; see ldms_sampler_base.man for the attributes of the base class.

.TP
.BR config
name=<plugin_name> stream=<stream>
.br
configuration line
.TP
name=<plugin_name>
.br
This MUST be dynamic_stream_sampler.
.TP
stream=<stream>
.br
Name of the stream to which to subscribe for the command messages. Currently tis MUST be cmd_streamPORTNO and these must be distinct per ldmsd in the system.
.RE

.SH BUGS
This is under development and is not guarenteed to work and is subject to change without notice.

There is a temporary functionality that has the final one send a message down the new stream.

There is a temporary functionality that also sets subscribers all the way in the plugins in order to test the new stream with the temporary message sent functionality.

There is an incomplete functionality that takes a tear down message, but there are some issues impeding completion.

There are some parameters that have yet to be passed in.


.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Within ldmsd_controller or a configuration file:
.nf
load name=dynamic_stream_sampler
config name=dynamic_stream_sampler producer=host1 instance=host1/dynamic_stream_sampler stream=cmd_stream52001 component_id=1
start name=dynamic_stream_sampler interval=1000000 offset=0
.fi

.PP
.nf
An external source will call the publisher like so:
> ldmsd_stream_publish -x sock -p 52001 -s cmd_stream52001 -t json -a munge -f cmdstream.in -h localhost

where cmdstream.in looks like:
{"cmd" : "SETUP_FEEDBACK", "stream" : "dynamicbar", "prdcrname" : "zedprdcr", "list" : "localhost@52001@cmd_stream52001:localhost@52002@cmd_stream52002:localhost@52003@cmd_stream52003"}
or
{"cmd" : "SETUP_FEEDBACK", "stream" : "dynamicbar", "prdcrname" : "zedprdcr", "list" : "localhost@52001@cmd_stream52001@sock@munge:localhost@52002@cmd_stream52002:localhost@52003@cmd_stream52003"}
Where
.TP
cmd=SETUP_FEEDBACK
.br
This must be SETUP_FEEDBACK
.TP
stream=<dynamic stream name>
.br
.TP
prdcrname=<upstream prdcrname used in the add_prdcr call to ldmsd_controller>
.br
.TP
list = <list>
.br
This is a list of hosts of the upstream L's, what port on which they sit,
what command stream they listen to, and optionally the host and auth (these default to localhost and munge).
They need a different command stream name
because the callback blocks to adding new subscribers on the same stream.
It is possible that we could do just the functionality here by sending the message up the stream to all the
plugins without the changing of the message, but it is written this way for greatest generality.

There is not complete robust-to-failure parsing.
.RE

.PP
In the log file of the ldmsd:
.nf
You will see data as it parses, sends, and receives, the maessages.
.ni

.PP
Also, if you call ldmsd_controller:
.nf
You will see the prdcr and streams get established. etc.

Note that the dynamic_streams sampler does not do a sample, instead it subscribes to the stream with a callback
and prints out what it got off the stream.
.fi


